Our model, in its current state, can take in the cartoon-made tank images and, from that, is able to disseminate whether it is a tank or not.
It has been able to do this so far with a 100% success rate, given images of vehicles ranging from tanks to various APCs and transport vehicles. 
At this current rate, given the small and very minimalistic dataset, it is easy to see how it can be successful, as there is a total of 40 objects,
with 6 being used for final evaluations. Some final test results show that, out of the 6 given images, it had 3 true positives and 3 true negatives,
along with a training vs. validation loss curve showing how, after about 60 run-throughs of the model, our validation and training get closer and 
closer to being more exact at about 10% to 15%, meaning our model is more confident in itself and more correct. However, when given a whole new set 
of 50 vehicles it had a 94% success rate meaning we need to expand our testing data .Improvements to be made would be 
greatly expanding the dataset to include even more types of vehicles and even civilian vehicles, as well as adding very specific vehicles to 
confuse the model, such as tracked APCs or IFVs, such as a Bradley. Overall, work done: Ilan Brejt was responsible for updating the final 
project guide, as well as creating the prompt to design the model and creating the write-up. Grant Stec utilized the prompt to create the model
utilizing Claude and, furthermore, conducted the testing of the model that resulted in the training vs. validation graph and the confusion matrix.
